On Tue, May 27, 2014 at 7:20 PM, selpa'i <seladwa@gmx.de> wrote:

>
> Yes, that's how it tends to work for FAhA. The question is just in what
> way "looking" can surround anything. I'd say it could work if the point of
> the "looker" is surrounded by a circle of the things being looked at.
> Another question on the page is what portion of a "circle" is needed around
> a thing for it to count as being surrounded.
>

A single quick direct look can hardly surround anything, but if there are
many lookers, or one looker moving around, or many things being looked at,
I don't see the problem. As for what counts as enough surrounding, usually
 looking is continuous, so if you start looking at one place, look around
and go back to look at the original place, the looking has most likely
fully surrounded something..

